---
// PORTABLE: canonical copy - ncs-astro-sanity-starter is the library of record for this file
/* ============================================================================
   Google Analytics 4 (gtag.js), loaded after the page is idle
   ============================================================================
   Foundation, edit with care.

   Rendered by Analytics.astro, and only when PUBLIC_GA_ID looks like a real
   Measurement ID. This file owns the tag; Analytics.astro owns the condition.
   They are separate files because prettier-plugin-astro cannot parse a
   <script> nested inside a template expression, so the script has to sit at
   the top level of a component of its own.

   TWO DELIBERATE DEPARTURES FROM GOOGLE'S SNIPPET. Both are load-bearing.
   Change either one only with a reason written down, because each was paid
   for by a real outage somewhere in this family (PORTS.md card 54).

   1. THE LIBRARY LOADS AFTER THE LOAD EVENT, NOT IN THE HEAD.
      Google's copy-paste block is `<script async src=".../gtag/js?id=...">`
      in the head, which puts roughly 100KB of third-party JavaScript in front
      of the render. Every repo in this family gates LCP as a HARD error, so
      that block is a gate failure waiting to happen. Instead the dataLayer and
      the gtag() stub are defined immediately, and the library itself is
      fetched at idle once the page has loaded. Anything that calls gtag()
      before it arrives is queued on the dataLayer, not lost, so no data is
      dropped. GA4 records the page view when the script arrives, a second or
      so later, and the reported figures are unchanged.

   2. THE <script> ELEMENT IS BUILT AT RUNTIME, NOT PRINTED AS A LITERAL TAG.
      Cloudflare Zaraz rewrites literal googletagmanager tags it finds in the
      HTML, takes over the gtag() call, and queues it into its own payload.
      When Zaraz then stops delivering, which it can do while still reporting a
      healthy config, the hits vanish with no error anywhere: no console
      message, no 5xx, no dashboard warning. Two sites in this family lost
      weeks of data to exactly that, and restoring a normal snippet did not fix
      it because the replacement got swallowed too. Creating the element in
      JavaScript means Zaraz's HTML rewriting never sees it. It costs nothing
      and it buys immunity, so it stays even on zones with no Zaraz today.

   The tag is intentionally absent from CI, Lighthouse and staging builds. See
   the comment on the Build step in .github/workflows/deploy.yml.
   ============================================================================ */

interface Props {
  /** The GA4 web data stream Measurement ID (G-XXXXXXXXXX), not the numeric property id. */
  gaId: string;
}

const { gaId } = Astro.props;
---

<script is:inline define:vars={{ gaId }}>
  window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
  // gtag.js inspects a real `arguments` object here, so rest parameters would
  // silently break every queued call. This shape is required, not stylistic.
  function gtag() {
    // eslint-disable-next-line prefer-rest-params
    window.dataLayer.push(arguments);
  }
  window.gtag = gtag;
  gtag('js', new Date());
  gtag('config', gaId);

  // Built as an element rather than printed as a tag. See departure 2 above.
  function loadGa() {
    var s = document.createElement('script');
    s.async = true;
    s.src = 'https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=' + encodeURIComponent(gaId);
    document.head.appendChild(s);
  }

  // Idle after load, with a timeout so a permanently busy page still reports.
  function whenIdle() {
    if ('requestIdleCallback' in window) requestIdleCallback(loadGa, { timeout: 4000 });
    else setTimeout(loadGa, 500);
  }

  if (document.readyState === 'complete') whenIdle();
  else window.addEventListener('load', whenIdle, { once: true });
</script>
